                        "review": "TL;DR for my fellow ADD folks: 10/10 survival loop, 0/10 narrative pacing. If you are too good at managing resources and beat the game efficiently, the story bottlenecks. You'll be locked out of essential nightly dialogues, forcing a jarring, unearned \"bad ending\" out of absolutely nowhere. Play slow, or prepare for whiplash! 😅\r\n\r\nOkay, well, as you can see, I've put a lot of time into this game. The problem isn't in the gameplay or the mechanics. I didn't realize the core flaw until I managed to complete a playthrough. I really wish Steam had a 'neutral' review option, because this game lands right in the middle. However, the negative taste left in my mouth from the ending is lingering, so I'm going with a thumbs down.\r\n\r\n• The Core Loop: The survival loop is very well handled. The art is extremely charming, and you will likely find the core family relatable, fun, and enjoy their interactions. The exploration, crafting, and character management are all tightly put together. For these reasons, I praise the makers and truly enjoyed the gameplay.\r\n\r\n• The 'Efficiency Penalty': Being too good at the game actively ruins the story. If you are looking for the 'good' ending, you have a massive backlog of dialogue to parse through. Because you only get one conversation per night, recruiting a lot of companions completely bottlenecks the system. If you survive too efficiently and beat the game before having these 100+ conversations, you will be locked out of crucial character arcs. Prioritizing the ones that get a satisfactory outcome would have been nice for the developer to do. 😅\r\n\r\n• Forced Epilogues: The text epilogues at the end are incredibly jarring and feel completely forced if you miss all of these conversation trees. Should you miss the dialogue queues, the game will suddenly tell you a couple's relationship was 'cold and distant', even if you never saw a single hint of that during your playtime. To avoid spoilers, there is also a specific character path you are supposed to run through, and if you miss it simply because of bottlenecked conversations, that character gets assassinated in the epilogue. I was absolutely caught off guard by all of it. 😅\r\n\r\nIn summary, it is a great survival game, but the narrative pacing and ending triggers are completely broken if you play efficiently. You are actively punished for doing well by missing the mandatory conversation paths that are strictly throttled to one per night.\r\n\r\n*sigh* Once I get over it I'm going to play the game again. I know now to take my time with it. I wanted to ensure anyone else wanting to get the most from the game know to do the same.😅",
